Monkey patching is a technique used in dynamic programming languages (like Python, JavaScript, and Ruby) to dynamically modify or extend the behavior of code at runtime without altering the original source files. It is highly useful for hotfixing, testing, and mocking. [1, 2, 3, 4, 5] 
## How It Works
Because classes and modules are mutable in dynamic languages, methods and attributes can be reassigned on the fly in memory. Once a patch is applied, any subsequent calls to the patched method will execute the new, custom logic instead of the original code. [2, 6, 7, 8] 
