## Common Use Cases

* Testing and Mocking: Replacing network-heavy API calls or random number generators with deterministic stubs to create consistent, repeatable unit tests. [6, 9] 
* Quick Hotfixes: Applying temporary fixes or security patches to third-party libraries without having to wait for upstream releases or maintaining a forked version of the library. [1, 2] 
* Adding Functionality: Appending custom logging, instrumentation, or new helper methods to existing, built-in classes like pandas.DataFrame or String. [6, 8] 

## Risks and Best Practices
While powerful, monkey patching is widely considered an anti-pattern or "hack" by many developers due to several major risks: [2, 10, 11, 12] 

* Global Impact: Modifying a class or global module in one place alters its behavior everywhere in the application, which can lead to unpredictable side effects and bugs.
* Maintainability: Overriding core behavior makes the codebase difficult for other developers to read and debug.
* Version Compatibility: A library update can change or break the underlying implementation you relied on for your patch. [2, 6, 13, 14] 

For a deeper dive into the caveats and when it's appropriate to use this technique, see the comprehensive community discussions on [Stack Overflow](https://stackoverflow.com/questions/5626193/what-is-monkey-patching).

