## Example in ruby:

Here is how monkey patching works in Ruby.
In Ruby, classes are never closed. You can open any existing class (even built-in ones like String or Integer) at any time and add or overwrite methods. [1, 2, 3, 4, 5] 
## Example 1: Extending a Built-in Class
This example adds a new method called yell to the standard String class.

# Open the existing String classclass String
  def yell
    "#{self.upcase}!!!"
  endend
# Use the new method on any string
puts "hello".yell # Output: HELLO!!!

## Example 2: Overriding Existing Behavior
This example modifies the built-in + method on the Integer class to alter how addition works.

# Open the Integer classclass Integer
  # Alias the original addition method to save it
  alias_method :original_plus, :+

  # Redefine the + method
  def +(other)
    self.original_plus(other).original_plus(1) # Adds 1 to the result
  endend

puts 2 + 2# Output: 5

## A Safer Ruby Alternative: Refinements
Because global monkey patching can break other parts of your app, Ruby introduced Refinements. These allow you to patch a class, but the changes are only active in the specific file or module where you explicitly activate them. [6, 7, 8, 9] 

module StringExtensions
  refine String do
    def whisper
      "#{self.downcase}..."
    end
  endend
# The patch is NOT active here# "SHOUT".whisper -> Raises NoMethodError
class Chat
  using StringExtensions # Activate the patch ONLY inside this class

  def speak
    puts "SECRET".whisper
  endend

Chat.new.speak# Output: secret...

